.TH "ZIPRUBYAPP" "1" "September 2026" ""
.SH "NAME"
\fBziprubyapp\fR \- Create executable Ruby script bundles using ZIP archives
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.nf
ziprubyapp [options] {directory | file \|\.\|\.\|\.}

Options:
  \-C, \-\-compress[=VAL]             compression level
  \-o, \-\-output=FILE                output file
  \-m, \-\-main=MOD                   name of main module to be loaded
  \-T, \-\-text\-archive               use text\-based archive format
  \-B, \-\-base64                     encode archive with BASE64
  \-D, \-\-provide\-data\-handle        provide DATA pseudo file\-handle
  \-I, \-\-includedir=DIR             library path to include
      \-\-[no\-]search\-includedir     search files within \-I directories
      \-\-[no\-]trim\-includedir       shorten file names for files in \-I directories
  \-\-help                           show help
.fi
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
This program bundles several Ruby module files and wraps them into an "executable" ZIP archive\. The output file can be invoked as a Ruby script, or (if a source file contains a "\fB#!\fR" line) executed directly as a command\. Additionally, it can be handled by almost any ZIP archiver as a self\-extracting ("SFX") file\.
.P
Inside Ruby scripts, the language's \fBrequire\fR facility is extended so that the program can simply use \fBrequire\fR or \fBrequire_relative\fR statements to load contained modules without modifying the \fB$:\fR (\fB$LOAD_PATH\fR) variable\.
.SH "OPTIONS"
.SS "ARGUMENTS"
.IP "\[ci]" 4
directory
.IP
If there is only one argument and it is the name of a directory, all \fB*\.rb\fR files under that directory (recursively) are included\. The directory path prefix itself is truncated\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
files
.IP
Otherwise, all files specified in the arguments are included\.
.IP "" 0
.SS "INPUT/OUTPUT OPTIONS"
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\-\-main, \-m
.IP
Specifies the main module that is automatically loaded\. A "shebang" line and consecutive comment lines are copied from the main module to the output\.
.IP
If a directory is specified as the argument and a \fB__main__\.rb\fR file exists at the root of that directory, that file is used by default\. Otherwise, the main module must be explicitly specified\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\-\-output, \-o
.IP
Specifies the name of the output file\.
.IP
If omitted, either the name of the source directory or the base name of the main module is used, with the extension '\fB\.rbz\fR' appended\.
.IP
Explicitly specifying the output file is always recommended\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\-\-includedir, \-I
.IP
Specifies locations to search for input files in addition to the current directory\. If this option is specified multiple times, directories are searched in the order specified\.
.IP
This option has two separate effects; for example, when '\fB\-Ilib file\.rb\fR' is specified on the command line:
.IP "\[ci]" 4
The command will include '\fBlib/file\.rb\fR' in the archive if '\fBfile\.rb\fR' does not exist in the working directory\. This behavior can be disabled by specifying '\fB\-\-no\-search\-includedir\fR'\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
The file '\fBlib/file\.rb\fR' will be included in the archive as '\fBfile\.rb\fR', trimming the library directory prefix\. This occurs whether the file is specified explicitly or via the \fB\-I\fR option\. This behavior can be disabled by specifying '\fB\-\-no\-trim\-includedir\fR'\.
.IP
If two or more files share the same name after trimming, the operation will fail with an error\.
.IP "" 0

.IP "" 0
.SS "ARCHIVE OPTIONS"
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\-\-compress, \-C
.IP
Specifies the compression level for the Deflate algorithm\.
.IP
If \fB\-C\fR is specified without a digit, the maximum compression level 9 is set\.
.IP
If omitted entirely, files are stored uncompressed\. This makes script content almost transparently readable\. Additionally, uncompressed scripts will not load \fBzlib\fR or other libraries at runtime\.
.IP
Outputs generated without the \fB\-C\fR option do not include decompression code, which means you must pass \fB\-0\fR (store) or similar flags if modifying the archive content with external ZIP tools\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\-\-base64, \-B
.IP
Encodes the embedded ZIP archive with Base64 encoding\. This increases script size by approximately 33% and loses ZIP\-transparent SFX behavior, in exchange for producing an ASCII\-clean script\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\-\-text\-archive, \-T
.IP
Uses a custom plaintext archive format for storing modules\. The output will not be compatible with standard ZIP archivers\.
.IP
Output scripts generated with this option will be plain text if all input modules are plain text in ASCII or ASCII\-compatible encodings\. Additionally, hand\-editing contents is easier because the format uses no binary structures\.
.IP
This format is useful when (1) embedded module sources need to be edited with text editors, or (2) source code must remain fully transparent for auditing or inspection (where even \fB\-C0\fR is insufficient)\.
.IP
Combining this option with \fB\-B\fR is supported, but not particularly useful\.
.IP "" 0
.SS "CONTENT HANDLING OPTIONS"
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\-\-provide\-data\-handle, \-D
.IP
Simulates the \fBDATA\fR file handle for the main module\. When enabled, it sets the \fBDATA\fR constant to a simulated pseudo\-file handle, providing script data located after the \fB__END__\fR token\.
.IP
If the main module does not contain the \fB__END__\fR token, this option is ignored\.
.IP
It is implemented via \fBStringIO\fR in Ruby\. For performance and simplicity, the relative position of the \fB__END__\fR token is recorded during generation\. Replacing the main module via external ZIP archivers will invalidate this data\.
.IP "" 0
.SS "OTHER OPTIONS"
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\-\-random\-seed
.IP
Specifies a seed integer for pseudorandom number generation\. Some features (e\.g\., \fB\-\-text\-archive\fR) use random numbers to generate unique byte sequences in the archive, causing outputs for identical inputs to vary over time\. Specifying a seed ensures deterministic output for identical input sets\. Note that this is not a strict guarantee; output may still vary slightly across different platform environments, machine architectures, or library versions\. The primary use case is ensuring reproducible archive outputs for version control systems such as Git or Subversion\.
.IP
In Ruby, seeds are 128\-bit integers\.
.IP "" 0
.SH "APIS"
There are currently no public APIs exposed to user scripts except import hooks\. The module \fBZipRubyApp\fR is provided within the zipped script\. If custom behavior is required upon packaging, guard clauses such as:
.IP "" 4
.nf
unless defined? ZipRubyApp
  $:\.unshift(__dir__)
end
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
can be used\.
.P
In Ruby, \fBrequire_relative\fR is recommended for loading modules located in the same directory as the script, and it integrates seamlessly with this tool\.
.SH "LIMITATIONS"
.IP "\[ci]" 4
Only pure Ruby scripts or modules can be loaded from ZIP archives\. Native extensions (\fB*\.so\fR, \fB*\.dll\fR) cannot be dynamically loaded\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
The \fB__FILE__\fR token in archived files will contain virtual path strings in the format "\fB_archivename_/_modulename_\fR", which do not exist on the real filesystem\. This also applies to the main script\. Consequently, the common "dual\-use" idiom \fBif __FILE__ == $0\fR will not function as expected\. Provide a dedicated entry script instead\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
For compactness (and to minimize dependencies to core libraries only), the embedded ZIP parser is extremely simplified\. It cannot parse archives utilizing advanced ZIP features or partially corrupted archives\. Exercise caution when modifying packed archives with external ZIP tools\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
All files are extracted into memory at program startup\. Avoid including unnecessary large files in the archive\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
Module loading is simulated using \fBKernel\.eval\fR, and \fBKernel\.require\fR is overridden to extend search logic\. While carefully implemented, unknown side effects may exist or behavior may change in future Ruby versions\. Unlike Python or Perl, Ruby lacks native hooks for extending module resolution\.
.IP "" 0
.SH "IMPLEMENTATION"
A ZIP archive containing module files is stored in the \fBDATA\fR section\. A minimal parser for ZIP archives is embedded in the output script to extract module source code into in\-memory storage at startup\. The \fBrequire\fR and \fBrequire_relative\fR methods in the \fBKernel\fR module are overridden to load these in\-memory modules\.
.SH "DEPENDENCIES"
Zipped scripts generated by this command do not depend on external gems or modules, relying solely on standard libraries included in core Ruby distributions (version 2\.3\.1 or later)\.
